# toon3d-story-2d fix log

The 2D-canvas version of toon3d-story (fake 3D drawn with canvas recipes), kept when toon3d-story moved to the 3D renderer on 2026-11-24 (renderer: three). History below is shared with toon3d-story up to epoch 2.


## epoch 0 (2026-11-24): created from a reference reel
Made by Claude from the rea-video breakdown of references/3d.mp4 (63.8 s, 720x1280, 30 fps): a toon-shaded 3D cartoon telling a true money anecdote with a twist, 26 shots (23.5 cuts a minute, mean 2.5 s, one 9.6 s ending hold), one fast male TTS narrator (3.4 words/s, about 135 Hz), one-word captions, 42 pop and ding effects, no music bed. Analysis: reference-lab/runs/3d/analysis-claude (survey and review answered by Claude, cg_3d on all 26 shots; the pixel detector had called most of them ai_video), crops in reference-lab/runs/3d/crops, committed copy in reference-lab/refs/3d.

What was taken from the reference:
- Look: simple 3D forms with smooth shading from one warm top-left light and dark warm ink outlines on everything (style.md: flat 71%, gradients 13%, outlines on 42% of hard edges, detail 8.3); ball-head characters (a sphere head about half the height, a small tube body, stick limbs ending in balls, dot or ring eyes, thick brow bars, stubble dots, sweat drops); warm lamp-lit interiors with a hanging cone lamp, a cream light pool and a strong vignette, two-tone walls (striped upper wall, rails, panelled wainscot), wood plank floors, rugs. Written as three drawing recipes (shaded sphere as a radial gradient, one-point-perspective box with back corners pulled toward a shared vanishing point, cylinder gradient) plus room geometry in px.
- Colour (oklch_colours.py on 9 crops + style.md): ink L 0.13 to 0.16 hue about 21 to 25; ground L 0.26 C 0.03; cream L 0.97 C 0.02 to 0.06 hue about 107; saturated red L 0.53 to 0.62 C 0.21 to 0.25 hue 30 against teal-green L 0.51 to 0.77 C 0.09 to 0.12 hue 150 to 180 (about 140 degrees apart); wood L 0.42 to 0.52 C 0.10 hue 45 to 63; amber room hue 61 to 83; screen green L 0.51 to 0.60 C 0.13 to 0.17 hue 145 to 150; highlighter yellow L 0.93 C 0.10 to 0.17 hue about 98. Contrast 14:1.
- Structure (script + review): outcome first in one sentence with its number, flashback with the year on a wall calendar, the odd object in a close-up with its number and fine print highlighted, the casual act, "a week later" plus a one-word reaction line, a question to the viewer answered with one word, the other side strikes back after a time jump (calendar flips), "you see" reveal, a deadpan punchline ending. Became the beats hook, setup, thing, act, surprise, question, twist, reveal, ending (topic stripped).
- Editing: wide, medium, reaction close-up, over-the-shoulder with the hero's head as a huge cropped sphere, two-shot behind a table, full-frame inserts of papers and screens. The engine allows 5 to 9 shots by default and each shot is one M3 call, so the template asks for 7 to 10 shots with 2 or 3 camera setups each (hard cuts on words inside a shot), which gives about 25 cuts a minute.
- Numbers on screen: the reference shows every number on a thing in the world (machine screen, statement, cheque with a yellow highlight, wall calendar); captions are only the spoken word. Became the INSERT panel (header, key number on a highlight bar, detail row, exact geometry) and the date card.
- Sound: narrator chris (casual American storyteller, measured 2.5 words/s on eleven-v4 in cache/voice/rates.json; the reference's TTS is faster at 3.4), tones per beat; no music in the reference, so feel Humorous Bouncy 95-115 bpm by kind with payoff_shot the reveal; effects dense (a pop on every insert, count, highlight and calendar flip, whoosh on a new place, knock on the twist), against _base's "a few accents only", because the reference has 42 in 64 s.
- Kept out (do_not_copy): the bank, the cheque, the cash machine, the safe-deposit room, the dollar figures, dates and every word on screen. The words "letter", "offer", "machine" in the beats are generic kinds of thing, not the reference's.

Checked before writing (scratchpad prototype rendered in the engine's own stage): the sphere recipe with a 7 px ink outline on a cream head, a cap clipped to the head's circle and a brim bar reads like the reference's characters; a striped wall with rails and a red panelled wainscot, plank lines to a vanishing point, a cone lamp with a radial pool and a vignette reads as the reference's room. The flat desk in the prototype looked 2D, hence the one-point-perspective box recipe.

Engine limits met (not changed; flagged for the main session):
- Captions: the reference's are one UPPERCASE word, heavy white with a black outline, about 76 px cap height, centred at y about 1410. The engine's "captions: words" is the closest (1 or 2 lowercase words, 96 px, white with a dark outline, y 1560). An uppercase option (and y about 1420) would match it.
- No 3D: characters are drawn in one front view; the over-the-shoulder view draws the back of the hero's head as a plain sphere in the shot, since poses have no back views.
- Whip pans inside a shot are not asked for (drawing the world several times per frame for the blur costs too much frame time); the engine's own smear at each shot start plays that role.
- The kit can only hand shots palette, font, bg, panel, title, post (kitApi): the sphere, box and cylinder recipes are written into look so kit, assets and shots each write them the same way, instead of a kit.ball helper that assets might call before it exists.
- The sprite-sheet reviewer is told to keep adult heads at a quarter to a third of the height; the assets section states the half-height head is the style so it is not "fixed".
- The reference has no music; the engine always lays a bed.

Train brief: "Bitcoin Pizza Day: the man who paid 10,000 bitcoin for two pizzas in 2010" (a true anecdote with a number, a time jump and a punchline; nothing in common with the reference's topic).

First run (btcpizza-e1, 2026-11-24) stopped at the voice step: ElevenLabs returned quota_exceeded (0 credits left). The plan passed on its 4th try (1 x digits in lines, 2 x a required beat dropped: with 8 shots M3 left out reveal, then ending). Fixes before the rerun: the narrator is named am_echo (Kokoro's casual American man, which the engine maps to chris on ElevenLabs; "chris" itself has no Kokoro mapping and would fall back to the British george); the structure says which beats go first when there are fewer shots (question, then act). Rerun with VOICE_MODEL=kokoro.

## epoch 1 (2026-11-24): fixes after reviewing btcpizza-e1 (Kokoro am_echo; 55.1 s for 50, 9 shots, MiniMax $0.380, reel total $0.380; 700 s wall; grade: rendering 0.03, rhythm 0.26, colour 0.26)
Epoch 0 got right: the look reads as the reference's toon 3D (shaded sphere heads with ink outlines, caps clipped to the head, perspective boxes, cone lamps, striped walls over panelled wainscot, plank floors to a vanishing point); rendering distance 0.03 (flat 0.78 vs 0.71, outlines 0.41 vs 0.42); 22.9 cuts a minute against the reference's 23.5, from 2 or 3 camera setups inside each shot; a working OTS with the cap seen from behind; the reaction take (ring eyes, open o mouth, sweat drops); insert panels with the number on the yellow bar and ball hands at the corners; the date card; word captions. Audio: am_echo 2.31 w/s, pitch spread 3.76 st (gate 4), music "Comic Plodding" fit, voice over music 13.2 dB.
Flaws -> fix:
- Colour washed out (ground lightness 0.56 vs 0.26, contrast 4.6 vs 14.1, analogous vs multi-hue): M3's palette was pastel (red chroma about 0.13, teal about 0.08, pinkish skin) and the rooms mostly tan and amber, with a weak vignette -> red named as the most saturated colour on screen, teal clear, skin a neutral cream never pink; tans at most a third of the frame; the person's room cool over warm, at most one amber room; ceiling from the ground colour down; floor darkening to L 0.30; vignette 65 to 75%; about 15% of each frame at ground lightness.
- Close-ups cut the face off (eyes at the top edge, a 190% hero rejected) and wides put the heads against the top edge -> CLOSE draws the hero outside the camera at an exact scale and centre (head 850 px across between y 250 and 1100); WIDE hero 650 to 800 px tall with the head top at y 420 to 600.
- Shot 3 (OTS toward a screen and inserts only) failed 3 times on "largest subject 0 px"; shot 8 redrew the hero by hand (grey blob mouth, a flat red ellipse) and shipped failing -> characters always via h.asset; every shot has a setup with a character asset 400 px or more; OTS always faces the supporting person.
- Shot 8 put a small document beside the face, half over it -> the insert is its own setup at the exact panel size, never beside or over a face.
- Stated amounts counted up (the offer counting 5,942 to 10,000 BTC, $40 to $41) -> only a number that changes in the story counts; a stated one pops in.
- Date card "MAY 2010" overlapped "22" (shot 2 shipped failing) and another card read "MAY 22, 2010" over "22" -> exact text centres and what goes where; never the same number twice.
- 3 character assets (buyer and a pizza worker as separate assets) -> exactly two character assets, the second plays everyone by role.
- Characters had a grey ear ball and long dangling legs and arms -> no ears, neck or nose; legs no longer than the body; hands never below the body.
- Story stiff and wrong: encyclopedia phrasing ("Jacksonville programmer Hanyecz", "novelty enthusiasts", "recognized as bitcoin's first notable retail purchase"), forty-one dollars said in 3 shots, and an invented reveal ("Hanyecz knew those valuable coins were worth vastly more") -> spoken like telling a friend, banned label stacks and encyclopedia words, each number once; the reveal is a true fact from the reference, never what someone knew or felt. (The fetched reference holds no later bitcoin price, so a "worth X today" punchline was not possible for this brief.)
- Brand on screen ("Two Papa John's") -> no brand names on screen.
- Motion low (frame change 0.82 vs 2.60, moving 58% vs 97%) -> push-in 8 to 12% per setup with a 20 to 40 px drift, character bob 6 to 10 px.
- Only 4 effects measured (plan had 2 per shot; reference 42) -> 3 or 4 per shot.
- Plan rejections: "ending" beat dropped once more (now 9 shots with all 7 required, so it was dropped while fixing the word budget), lines 119 for 111 once.
Engine notes: ElevenLabs had 0 credits (Kokoro used). Captions still lowercase 1 to 2 words at y 1560 (reference: one uppercase word at y about 1410).

## epoch 2 (2026-11-24): fixes after reviewing btcpizza-e2 (Kokoro am_echo; 48.5 s for 50, 8 shots, MiniMax $0.367, reel total $0.367; 997 s wall; grade: rendering 0.04, rhythm 0.18, colour 0.25) and the user's review
Epoch 1 got right: saturated rooms (red striped walls over teal wainscot, cream wall over dark teal), darker corners, 23.5 cuts a minute (same as the reference), whole heads in close-ups, exactly two characters plus one machine, inserts of full size, no counting of stated amounts, the date card text no longer overlapping. The grader still reads ground lightness 0.52 (0.26) and contrast 4.3 (14.1).
User: less movement and fewer effects than the reference are fine for a 3D reel; the focus is animation and correctness: "the drawings overlap on the counter, the person behind the counter is drawn over the counter".
Flaws -> fix:
- Characters stand ON counters, desks and tables (feet on the top face, legs over the front) in e1 shots 4, 5, 9 and e2 shots 1, 6, 7, 8. Two causes found in the code: kit.bg painted a counter, a stool and crates out on the open floor (bg_render of pizza_counter), so everything placed later lands on top of them; and the shots drew their furniture first and then placed characters with feet inside the furniture's top face (shot_8.js: counter box at y 1160, characters' feet at about y 1214, drawn after). -> kit.bg is the room's shell only (walls, floor, rug, lamp, things against the back wall, nothing below y 1250); furniture is drawn by the shots; a depth section in shots: every standing thing gets a floor y, draw sorted by it, the exact "behind a counter" placement (centre y = top - 0.12 * Hb * s, front face at least 0.45 * Hb * s tall, character drawn before the furniture), sitting at a desk, in front of furniture, two characters never overlapping, props resting on surfaces, on the floor or at a hand, nothing floating. Director flags these first.
- A phone floating at the chin (e1 shot 6), a red stool hanging in the foreground (e2, from kit.bg) -> the props rules above.
- The template's required twist beat made M3 invent events for a story without one (e1: "Hanyecz knew"; e2 v1: a 2013 "regulatory review" with "transaction flagged", "47 pizzas", a "present value" of 100,000,000 in bitcoins; the director replaced some, e2 still ships "by twenty-thirteen, US agencies were seizing bitcoins" and a "MAY 2010 / 10" card on a 2013 line) -> twist, question and act are used only when the reference has the real event; required beats are hook, setup, thing, surprise, reveal, ending; 6 to 10 shots; every number and date on screen must be stated in the reference, with the right unit; no banners or labels in the room.
- Reverted epoch 1's extra motion and effects (push-in back to 4 to 6%, no drift or bob; effects 1 or 2 per shot), per the user.
